All 36 tests pass, including `Cp26Tests` (the new per-country length rule) with no regressions.

## What I did

The telephone was already normalized to E.164 form in `OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeTelephone`, but it only enforced the generic "8–15 digits" rule. I added a per-country national-number length check.

**`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** — after the number is normalized to its digit string, it now also validates the national-number length against the country code, returning `null` (which the `addOwner`/`updateOwner` handlers turn into a `400 Bad Request`) when the length is wrong:

```java
private boolean hasValidNationalLength(String digits) {
    if (digits.startsWith("61")) {
        return digits.length() - 2 == 9;   // +61 requires 9 national digits
    }
    if (digits.startsWith("1")) {
        return digits.length() - 1 == 10;  // +1 requires 10 national digits
    }
    return true;                           // no rule for other country codes (e.g. +64)
}
```

This rejects `+61 123` (only 3 national digits) with 400 as the spec requires, while leaving valid AU numbers (`0412 345 678` → `+61412345678`, 9 national digits) and rule-less country codes like `+64` untouched.

